# LRN Specification

This document defines the formal structure and intent of a Learned Runtime Node (LRN).

## Definition

An LRN is:

validated operational knowledge that can be reused by an ANS during runtime.

This definition separates LRN from:
- a memory note;
- a raw prompt;
- a private model artifact;
- an unverified observation;
- a permanent rule.

An LRN must distinguish at least the following stages of existence:
- observation;
- proposal;
- implementation;
- validation;
- activation.

## Required structure

Each LRN may contain the following fields:

```yaml
lrn_id: LRN-0001
version: v0.1.0
title: Example title
description: Short description of the observed pattern and intended use
creator:
  id: CR-0001
  name: Creator Name
  organization: Organization or TBD

domain: example-domains
authority: TBD
scope:
  environment: TBD
  conditions: TBD
  limitations: TBD
trigger: TBD
context: TBD
procedure: TBD
pattern: TBD
recommendation: TBD
expected_outcome: TBD
evidence:
  observations: []
  experiments: []
  historical_cases: []
  references: []
provenance: TBD
dependencies: []
validation:
  status: proposed
  validators: []
  validation_events: []
  last_review: TBD
  next_review: TBD
negative_evidence: []
known_failures: []
confidence: TBD
rollback: TBD
retirement_condition: TBD
created_at: 2026-01-01T00:00:00Z
updated_at: 2026-01-01T00:00:00Z
```

## Field meanings

### lrn_id
A stable identity string such as `LRN-0001`.

### version
Version identifier. Example: `v0.1.0`. A significant change produces a new version, not silent overwrite.

### title
Short human-readable title.

### description
What the LRN is intended to do, where it applies, and why it matters.

### creator
Identity and provenance details of the proposer.

Fields:
- `id`: creator identity or registry identifier
- `name`: human-readable creator name
- `organization`: organization, team, or `TBD`

### domain
The domain or area of behavior the LRN addresses.

### scope
The operational boundary of the LRN.

Required nested fields:
- `environment`: operating environment or context
- `conditions`: required conditions or assumptions
- `limitations`: explicit limits and exclusions

### trigger
The event, condition, or signal that causes the knowledge to be considered or applied.

### context
The broader operational or decision context in which the LRN is valid.

### procedure
A concise description of the operational procedure to be followed.

### pattern
The pattern, condition, or recurring structure identified by the creator.

### recommendation
What the system or operator should do after recognizing the trigger and context.

### expected_outcome
The expected effect or operational result under the defined scope.

### evidence
Evidence, results, and source material used to justify the proposal.

Nested fields:
- `observations`: observed facts or recurring evidence
- `experiments`: tests or structured experiments
- `historical_cases`: prior case references or analogues
- `references`: documents, citations, or source links

### provenance
The lineage of the LRN: source of origin, version history, related records, or process marks.

### dependencies
Other LRNs, data artifacts, services, or environmental assumptions required for safe usage.

### validation
Validation status and review history.

Nested fields:
- `status`: lifecycle status such as `proposed`, `testing`, `validated`, `active`, `suspended`, `retired`
- `validators`: list of validator identifiers or roles
- `validation_events`: list of validation record references or IDs
- `last_review`: timestamp or TBD
- `next_review`: timestamp or TBD

### negative_evidence
A structured list of failures, counterexamples, contradictions, and exceptions relevant to the LRN.

### known_failures
Documented failure modes, tasks or scenarios where the LRN does not perform as expected.

### confidence
A qualitative or quantitative confidence indicator for the current evidence set, but not a claim of permanent truth.

### rollback
Procedures or conditions required to revert or neutralize the knowledge if new evidence emerges.

### retirement_condition
The condition under which the LRN should be suspended or retired.

### created_at
ISO-8601 timestamp when the LRN record was created.

### updated_at
ISO-8601 timestamp when the LRN was last modified.

## Lifecycle contract

A valid LRN is expected to preserve the relationship between:
- observation
- proposal
- validation
- activation
- monitoring
- revalidation

The repository requires that an LRN may not be considered active without a recorded validation event.

## Governance requirement

The repository does not treat validation as permanent truth. Validation is an operational state based on current available evidence.

## Non-goals

This specification does not claim that any LRN is universally correct, scientifically accepted, or institutionally certified.

If a fact is not available, use `TBD`, `NOT YET VALIDATED`, or `UNVERIFIED`.

